WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.058
A smooth drug-release curve on a computer can be

00:00:04.058 --> 00:00:07.666
produced before a single tablet or vesicle is tested.

00:00:07.666 --> 00:00:09.270
Its smoothness proves nothing.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:21.837
A physical dosage form releases medicine over time. A

00:00:21.837 --> 00:00:25.249
mathematical model tries to describe that changing

00:00:25.249 --> 00:00:25.737
process.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:35.439
Formulation composition, geometry and transport

00:00:35.439 --> 00:00:39.027
assumptions affect simulated release. The selected

00:00:39.027 --> 00:00:42.614
equations embody choices that need scrutiny.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:51.667
The book chapter examines simulation of drug release

00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:55.356
and performance prediction using virtual models within

00:00:55.356 --> 00:00:56.411
pharmaceutical development.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.688
Without calibration and suitable validation, a model

00:01:05.688 --> 00:01:09.389
can produce elegant curves and still make poor

00:01:09.389 --> 00:01:09.852
predictions.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:20.996
Assumptions about transport or kinetics need

00:01:20.996 --> 00:01:25.133
experimental comparison. A convincing digital twin must

00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:27.497
expose residuals and uncertainty.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.579
Mechanistic assumptions, parameter uncertainty and

00:01:35.579 --> 00:01:39.333
extrapolation beyond known experiments all limit

00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:41.836
credible digital performance forecasts.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.913
Models earn their place in formulation science when

00:01:49.913 --> 00:01:53.376
they guide better tests and can fail against

00:01:53.376 --> 00:01:53.809
observations.
